From: Bitterblue Smith <rtl8821cerfe2@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: Fix the unaligned 32-bit access to REG_TXPAUSE

Backported from OpenWrt backports 6.12.96 (this tree pins 6.12.61, which
predates it). Verified by extracting backports-6.12.96.tar.zst and diffing
rtw_core_enable_beacon(); the result of this patch is byte-identical to the
6.12.96 source.

REG_TXPAUSE is 0x0522 and is a single byte, but rtw_core_enable_beacon() used
rtw_write32_set()/rtw_write32_clr() on it. Those are read-modify-write helpers,
so a 32-bit access at 0x0522 spans 0x0522..0x0525 and therefore also reads back
and rewrites REG_RD_CTRL (0x0524) as collateral on every beacon enable/disable.

On PCIe hosts this is additionally an unaligned MMIO access, which faults on
ARM64. On USB it does not fault - the address is passed to the device in the
vendor control request - but the collateral read-modify-write of REG_RD_CTRL
still happens, which is a real functional bug.

This matters for the Raspberry Pi 5 USB Wi-Fi AC path specifically:
rtw_core_enable_beacon() is AP-only ("if (!rtwdev->ap_active) return;") and is
called from the scan / channel-switch C2H handler in fw.c, i.e. exactly during
the HT_SCAN phase of hostapd AP bring-up.

Use the 8-bit accessors so only REG_TXPAUSE is touched.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c
@@ -2433,10 +2433,10 @@
 
 	if (enable) {
 		rtw_write32_set(rtwdev, REG_BCN_CTRL, BIT_EN_BCN_FUNCTION);
-		rtw_write32_clr(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
+		rtw_write8_clr(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
 	} else {
 		rtw_write32_clr(rtwdev, REG_BCN_CTRL, BIT_EN_BCN_FUNCTION);
-		rtw_write32_set(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
+		rtw_write8_set(rtwdev, REG_TXPAUSE, BIT_HIGH_QUEUE);
 	}
 }
 
